Technical Specification V2: Spaceware – Proxima Descent (Phase Completion Module)

1. Current Context

The game already has a functional loop: the Janax-M1 spacecraft starts at 40,000 altitude and must descend to 0 while dodging SVG-generated asteroids. The player controls descent with lateral movement and/or braking. The core mechanic is an inverse infinite runner: instead of running forward, you fall downward.

2. What Must Be Built

After reaching altitude 0, the game must not end. It must transition to a second phase with an animated cutscene and a new game state.

2.1 Trigger Condition

· When altitude = 0 (surface collision achieved successfully, not by hitting an obstacle).
· The altitude counter stops decrementing.
· Asteroid generation and ship movement are paused.

2.2 Transition Screen (Immediate)

Display a centered message:

```
MISSION PHASE 1 COMPLETED
JANAX-M1 LANDED ON PROXIMA B
```

Below it, an interactive button: CONTINUE TO PHASE 2

The player must click or tap the button to proceed. No timeout. The game does not advance until user interaction.

2.3 Animated Cutscene (Pure SVG, No External Video)

When "CONTINUE TO PHASE 2" is pressed:

1. Message and button disappear (quick fade out, 0.2s).
2. Camera zooms in slightly on the ship (1.1x zoom over 1 second).
3. SVG landing animation:
   · The Janax-M1 ship moves slowly downward (final descent).
   · The ground (a horizontal line with crater texture, generated in SVG) appears and stabilizes.
   · Dust particles (small white circles) burst from the ship's base.
   · The ship tilts slightly (rotation -2° to +2°) then levels out.
4. Background text: "Proxima Centauri B – Surface" fades in at the bottom (0% → 100% over 1s).
5. Total cutscene duration: 3–4 seconds.

2.4 After the Cutscene Ends

· The ship remains static in the center of the screen.
· The background changes from black to a simple SVG surface landscape (rocky ground, dim sky with a distant star).
· A new HUD appears: Phase 2 (oxygen bar, resource counter, etc.).
· The game transitions into collection and construction mode (Spaceware tycoon, already exists).

2.5 Technical Requirements for Rezona

· The cutscene must be 100% code-generated SVG. No external videos or GIFs.
· No external JavaScript libraries. Everything must run inside Rezona's engine (inline TypeScript/JS is allowed).
· Animation must be smooth (60 fps).
· The "Continue" button must work on touch (mobile) and click (desktop).
· Game state must persist: ship position, asteroids, altitude. Nothing resets.

3. Integration with Existing Game

Current Component Required Extension
Descent loop (40k → 0) Already exists. Stops at altitude = 0.
Asteroid generation Freezes at altitude = 0. No new asteroids spawn.
Ship controls Disabled during cutscene. Reactivated in Phase 2.
Altitude variable Hidden after Phase 1.
Game phase variable Changes from "DESCENT" to "LANDING_CUTSCENE" to "SURVIVAL".

4. Example User Flow

1. You play the normal descent. Dodge asteroids. Reach 0.
2. "MISSION PHASE 1 COMPLETED" appears, along with the "CONTINUE TO PHASE 2" button.
3. You press the button.
4. The ship lands in an SVG animation. Dust. Tilts. Stabilizes.
5. The game changes: now you collect resources, build upgrades, manage oxygen.
6. End of specification.

5. Expected Deliverable

· Modified game code (only the transition and cutscene part).
· Key documented functions: onAltitudeZero(), showPhaseCompleteScreen(), playLandingAnimation(), switchToPhaseTwo().
· The cutscene must be reproducible in Rezona with no external dependencies.

6. Public Repository Purpose

This specification is part of a public archive of Rezona game specs. Anyone can:

· Copy the prompt and paste it into Rezona's AI.
· Use an AI scraper to extract the specification and adapt it to their own game.
· Learn how to write professional game specifications instead of lazy prompts.

The goal is not to give away finished games. The goal is to teach the method of specification-driven development inside Rezona.

